-- title:
--   Pinned Rankin–Selberg niceness for a cubic base change
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $K$ is a number field equipped with an integral algebra structure over $\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$ and with $[K:\mathbb Q]=3$ (hypothesis `_hdeg`), and $\Phi$ is a Hecke eigensystem over $\mathbb Q$ with complex coefficients, that is, a non-zero level ideal $\Phi.\mathrm{level}\subseteq\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$ together with two families $\Phi.a,\Phi.b$ indexed by the finite places of $\mathbb Q$.
--
--   The data and hypotheses are as follows. (i) `hΦ`: $\Phi$ is arithmetically genuinely cusp-realizable at the general production pins `productionPinsGeneral ℚ`, i.e. the rescaled eigensystem $\Phi.\mathrm{toRawCentral}$ (same level and same $a$, with $b_v$ replaced by $(\mathrm{N}v)^{-1}\Phi.b_v$) admits a smooth cusp realization at those pins which is a genuine cusp realization. (ii) A finite set $S_{\mathbb Q,0}$ of finite places of $\mathbb Q$ with $\|\Phi.b_p\|=1$ for all $p\notin S_{\mathbb Q,0}$ (`hb`), and (`ha`) summability of $p\mapsto\|\Phi.a_p\|\,(\mathrm{N}p)^{-\sigma}$ for every real $\sigma>1$. (iii) A real archimedean parameter $P$, either `RealArchParam.principal u₁ a₁ u₂ a₂` with $u_1,u_2\in\mathbb C$ and $a_1,a_2\in\mathbb Z/2$, or `RealArchParam.discrete u₀ n hn` with $n\ge 1$. (iv) A finite set $S$ of finite places of $\mathbb Q$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hlink` (the Whittaker link) asserts the existence of a smooth cusp realization $R$ at the general pins for $\Phi.\mathrm{toRawCentral}$ with continuous underlying function, and of a function $C$ on $\mathbb A_{\mathbb Q,\mathrm{fin}}\times \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb A_{\mathbb Q})$, such that: $R.\mathrm{exceptionalSet}\subseteq S$; $C(1,1)\neq 0$; at every real place $w$ of $\mathbb Q$, if $P=\mathrm{principal}(u_1,a_1,u_2,a_2)$ then $|\mathrm{Re}(u_1-u_2)|<1$, and for every non-zero integer $p$ with $u_1-u_2=p$ one has $a_1-a_2\neq p+1$ in $\mathbb Z/2$; at every real place $w$, the central character of $R$, transported along the identification of the top subgroup with the full idele unit group, satisfies `IsArchCompAt` at $w$ with exponent $P.\mathrm{centralExponent}+1$ and integer $P.\mathrm{centralSign}.\mathrm{val}$, where `IsArchCompAt K μ w u a` means that the archimedean local component of $\mu$ at $w$ is $x\mapsto \|x\|^{\mathrm{mult}(w)u}(x/\|x\|)^{a}$; and, for every parity assignment $\mathrm{par}$ on the infinite places of $\mathbb Q$, there are a function $\varphi$ on $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb A_{\mathbb Q})$, a family $W_r$ of complex functions indexed by the infinite places, and integer weights $k$, subject to twelve clauses: $\varphi$ is an isotypic cusp form at the production pins with central character $R.\mathrm{centralChar}$, level $\Phi.\mathrm{level}$, exceptional set $S$ and eigensystem $\Phi$ (smooth cuspidal automorphic with that central character, continuous, right invariant under the level subgroup, Hecke eigenfunction with eigenvalue $\Phi.a_v$ off $S$, and central eigenfunction with eigenvalue $\Phi.\mathrm{toRawCentral}.b_v$ off $S$); $\varphi\neq 0$; at every finite place $p$ of $\mathbb Q$ the local Whittaker space of $\varphi$ with respect to the standard additive character `psiQ` (the span of the local Whittaker functions of the right translates of $\varphi$) is generated by any of its non-zero elements under right translation, is admissible in the sense that for each open subgroup $U$ of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb Q_p)$ the $U$-invariants are contained in the span of a finite set of functions, and consists of vectors each of which is right invariant under some open subgroup; $\varphi$ is reproduced by right convolution with some factorizable test function $\alpha$; at every real place $w$, $\varphi$ has archimedean character `archWeightCharAt hw (k w)`, the $(k\,w)$-th power of the weight-one character of the archimedean row-isometry group at $w$, in the sense of the predicate `HasArchCharacterAt₀`; if $P=\mathrm{principal}(u_1,a_1,u_2,a_2)$ then $k\,w=\mathrm{signShift}(a_1+\mathrm{par}\,w)+\mathrm{signShift}(a_2+\mathrm{par}\,w)$, where $\mathrm{signShift}(a)$ is $0$ for $a=0$ and $1$ otherwise, while if $P=\mathrm{discrete}(u_0,n)$ then $k\,w=n+1$; the Whittaker coefficient of $\varphi$ at $1$ factorizes, in the sense that for every idele unit $a$ and every $g$ in the finite adelic subgroup (the kernel of the archimedean projection) the value at $\mathrm{diag}(a,1)\,g$ equals $\bigl(\prod_w W_r\,w(a_w)\bigr)\cdot C(a_{\mathrm{fin}},g)$, the archimedean components being taken through the embeddings $w.\mathrm{Completion}\to\mathbb C$; if $P=\mathrm{principal}(u_1,a_1,u_2,a_1)$ and $\mathrm{par}\,w=a_1$ then $W_r\,w(-t)=(-1)^{a_1.\mathrm{val}}W_r\,w(t)$ for all real $t$; if $P$ is discrete then $W_r\,w(t)=0$ for $t<0$; if $P=\mathrm{principal}(u_1,a_1,u_2,a_1)$ and $\mathrm{par}\,w=a_1+1$ then there is $s_0$ such that for $\mathrm{Re}\,s>s_0$ the Mellin transform of $t\mapsto (W_r\,w(t)+(-1)^{a_1.\mathrm{val}}W_r\,w(-t))/t$ converges and equals $\frac{2s+u_1+u_2-1}{4\pi}$ times the archimedean factor of the twist of $P$ by $(0,a_1)$; and for every real $w$ and every $b$ equal to $\mathrm{par}\,w$ or to $\mathrm{par}\,w+P.\mathrm{centralSign}$ there is $s_0$ such that for $\mathrm{Re}\,s>s_0$ the same Mellin transform (with $b$ in place of $a_1$) converges and equals the archimedean factor of the twist of $P$ by $(0,b)$.
--
--   Finally, $T_q$ is a finite set of finite places of $\mathbb Q$ and $\omega$ a character of the idele units of $K$ with values in $\mathbb C^{\times}$ which is an admissible twist (`hω`: trivial on principal ideles, continuous and unitary) and which (`hωT`) is unramified at every prime $\mathfrak P$ of $K$ whose restriction to $\mathbb Q$ lies outside $T_q$, with $\omega$ of a uniformizer idele at $\mathfrak P$ equal to the $b$-value of the formal base change of $\Phi$ to $K$, namely $\Phi.b_{\mathfrak P\cap\mathbb Q}^{\,f(\mathfrak P)}$.
--
--   The conclusion asserts the existence of a finite set $S_{\mathbb Q}$ of finite places of $\mathbb Q$ containing $S_{\mathbb Q,0}$, and of a finite set $S_K$ of finite places of $K$, such that $\mathfrak P\in S_K$ if and only if the place of $\mathbb Q$ below $\mathfrak P$ lies in $S_{\mathbb Q}$, and $\|\Phi.b_p\|=1$ for all $p\notin S_{\mathbb Q}$; together with a family $\mathrm{epsS}$ of characters $\mathrm{epsS}\,v$ of the local units at each finite place $v$ of $K$, and two functions $A,A^{\vee}$ on $S_K\to\mathbb Z$, satisfying the following ten conjuncts.
--
--   First, every prime of $K$ lying over a place in $T_q$ belongs to $S_K$. Second, at every real place $w$ of $K$, with $\mathrm{archOfParamR}\,K\,P\,w=P$: if $P=\mathrm{principal}(u_1,a_1,u_2,a_2)$ then for every non-zero integer $p$ with $u_1-u_2=p$ one has $a_1-a_2\neq p+1$ in $\mathbb Z/2$. Third, at every complex place $w$ of $K$, with $\mathrm{archOfParamC}\,K\,P\,w=P.\mathrm{baseChange}$ of components $u_1,k_1,u_2,k_2$: for all integers $p,q\ge 1$ neither $\bigl(2(u_1-u_2)=p+q$ and $k_1-k_2=p-q\bigr)$ nor $\bigl(2(u_1-u_2)=-(p+q)$ and $k_1-k_2=q-p\bigr)$ holds. Fourth, at every real place $w$ of $K$, $\omega$ satisfies `IsArchCompAt` with exponent $P.\mathrm{centralExponent}$ and integer $P.\mathrm{centralSign}.\mathrm{val}$. Fifth, at every complex place $w$ of $K$, $\omega$ satisfies `IsArchCompAt` with exponent the central exponent $u_1+u_2$ and integer the central twist $k_1+k_2$ of $P.\mathrm{baseChange}$. Sixth, $\mathrm{epsS}\,v$ is continuous for every $v\in S_K$. Seventh, $A$ and $A^{\vee}$ are uniformly bounded: there is a real $C$ with $\|A\,n\|\le C$ and $\|A^{\vee}n\|\le C$ for all $n$. Eighth, their supports are bounded below: there is $n_0$ such that $A\,n=A^{\vee}n=0$ whenever $n\,v<n_0\,v$ for some $v$. Ninth, $A\neq 0$.
--
--   Tenth, for every admissible twist $\mu$ of $K$ such that $\mathrm{localChar}\,\mu\,v\,(u)\cdot \mathrm{epsS}\,v\,(u)=1$ for all $v\in S_K$ and all local units $u$ of valuation $1$, and for all archimedean data $u_{\mathbb R},a_{\mathbb R}$ at the real places and $u_{\mathbb C},k_{\mathbb C}$ at the complex places such that $\mu$ satisfies `IsArchCompAt` at each real $w$ with exponent $u_{\mathbb R}(w)$ and integer $a_{\mathbb R}(w).\mathrm{val}$ and at each complex $w$ with exponent $u_{\mathbb C}(w)$ and integer $k_{\mathbb C}(w)$, the predicate `IsNicePinned` holds for the Rankin–Selberg datum $\mathrm{rsDatum}\,\mathbb Q\,S_{\mathbb Q}\,\Phi.a\,\Phi.b$ with local parameter $\mathfrak P\mapsto \mu(\text{uniformizer idele at }\mathfrak P)$ when $\mu$ is unramified at $\mathfrak P$ and $0$ otherwise, and with the four $\Gamma$-multisets $\mathrm{twistedGammaR}$ and $\mathrm{twistedGammaC}$ formed from the constant families $w\mapsto P$ (real places) and $w\mapsto P.\mathrm{baseChange}$ (complex places) twisted by $(u_{\mathbb R},a_{\mathbb R})$ and $(u_{\mathbb C},k_{\mathbb C})$, respectively from the duals of those parameters twisted by $(-u_{\mathbb R},a_{\mathbb R})$ and $(-u_{\mathbb C},-k_{\mathbb C})$; the two Dirichlet-type factors being $\mathrm{sPart}\,K\,S_K\,A\,\mu$ and $\mathrm{sPartDual}\,K\,S_K\,A^{\vee}\mu$, the sign being $\mathrm{pinnedRootNumber}$ of the formal base change of $\Phi$, of $\mu$, of $S_K$ and of the above archimedean families, and the conductor being $\mathrm{finiteConductor}\,K\,\mu\,S_K$. Unfolded, this datum is indexed by the finite places $p\notin S_{\mathbb Q}$, has norms $\mathrm{N}p$, Euler factors $\mathrm{rsEulerPoly}$ in $(\Phi.a_p,\Phi.b_p)$ and the three induced local parameters of $\mu$ at $p$, dual Euler factors the corresponding polynomials in $(\Phi.a_p/\Phi.b_p,\Phi.b_p^{-1})$ and the induced parameters of $\mu^{-1}$, abscissa $1$, centre $1/2$ and degree $6$; and `IsNicePinned` states that the datum is well formed and convergent, that the conductor is positive, and that there exist $\Lambda,\Lambda^{\vee}$ differentiable on all of $\mathbb C$ and bounded on vertical strips with $\Lambda(s)=\mathrm{sPart}(s)\cdot(\text{archimedean factor})(s)\cdot L(s)$ and $\Lambda^{\vee}(s)=\mathrm{sPartDual}(s)\cdot(\text{dual archimedean factor})(s)\cdot L^{\vee}(s)$ for $\mathrm{Re}\,s>1$, and $\Lambda(s)=\varepsilon\,N^{1/2-s}\,\Lambda^{\vee}(1-s)$ for all $s\in\mathbb C$.
--
--   This is the analytic input for the converse-theorem step in the Langlands–Tunnell argument: the completed twisted Rankin–Selberg $L$-function of the formal base change of $\Phi$ to a cubic field $K$ continues to an entire function, bounded on vertical strips, with the expected functional equation, for all admissible twists matching a prescribed family of local characters, the archimedean parameters being pinned to the base-change family of a single real parameter $P$. It is used by [`LanglandsTunnell.exists_isNicePinned_twistedDatum_formalBaseChange_archOfParam_superset_generic_of_whittaker_factorization_of_norm_eq_one_of_summable_of_localSpaceAt`](thm.html#LanglandsTunnell.exists_isNicePinned_twistedDatum_formalBaseChange_archOfParam_superset_generic_of_whittaker_factorization_of_norm_eq_one_of_summable_of_localSpaceAt).
